CORE
JmlListLibrary
--function "JmlListLibrary.useAdd:()I" --axiomatic-collections --unwind 5 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for axiomatic List models: ArrayList /
LinkedList / AbstractList for <init>, size, isEmpty, add,
get.

Lowering pipeline mirrors HashMap / HashSet:

  * LIST_INIT (ArrayList<init>, LinkedList<init>) →
    _sz[receiver] := 0.
  * LIST_SIZE / LIST_IS_EMPTY → _sz[receiver] (or
    `_sz[receiver] == 0`).
  * LIST_GET(i) → _list[(receiver << 32) | uint32(i)].
  * LIST_ADD(o) → _list[(receiver << 32) | uint32(_sz[receiver])] := o;
                  _sz[receiver]++; return true.

Element storage uses a separate `java::axiomatic::_list`
global, distinct from HashMap's `_kv` to avoid index
collisions when the same receiver pointer is reinterpreted
across collection types. The size array `_sz` is shared
across HashMap, HashSet, and List — its key is the
receiver's pointer-bit-pattern, which is unique per
allocation.

The test method `useAdd` allocates a fresh ArrayList, adds
"hello", and reads back size() == 1. With the model in
place this verifies under --axiomatic-collections.

JML resolver hookup
-------------------
The library-call branch in resolve_jml_expr already
recognises the size, isEmpty, contains, get, containsKey
method names; the receiver-shape predicate is widened in
parallel to also accept java.util.List, java.util.ArrayList,
java.util.LinkedList, java.util.AbstractList. With those,
JML clauses like `requires l.size() > 0;` resolve the same
way they do for HashMap and HashSet.

What this does NOT yet handle:
  * List.contains(Object) — needs an existential
    quantifier over [0, _sz[receiver]). Skipped for now.
  * List.remove, List.clear, List.set, indexOf, lastIndexOf.
  * Iterator.hasNext / next — separate work.
